# Future Directions

The unrestricted observer-count conjectures need a nondegeneracy condition: `one_observer_representation` proves that every topology is already represented by one observer who sees the real topology. Consequently, non-metrizability alone cannot force three observers, and the proposed lower bound for the Zariski topology on `ℝ²` is false under the stated definition.

A meaningful refinement could require every observer topology to be strictly finer than the real topology, require observers to belong to a prescribed class, or minimize observers among pairwise distinct proper refinements. Under such a revised definition, useful next questions are:

- characterize spaces that are the common-open-set topology of two strict refinements;
- determine whether separation or countability axioms are preserved by commonTopology;
- construct and study observer decompositions of algebraic Zariski topologies;
- extend the lower/upper-limit construction from `ℝ` to suitable densely ordered topological spaces;
- define indexed common topologies and prove universal properties for arbitrary observer families.
